This model merges all records and has no active snapshots. Under those assumptions it can omit keys whose newest version is a tombstone. A partial production compaction cannot assume that it has seen every old value.

```python
def compact_all(records: list[tuple[str, int, str | None]]) -> dict[str, str]:
    newest: dict[str, tuple[int, str | None]] = {}
    for key, version, value in records:
        if key not in newest or version > newest[key][0]:
            newest[key] = (version, value)
    return {key: value for key, (_, value) in sorted(newest.items())
            if value is not None}

records = [("a", 1, "old"), ("b", 1, "keep"),
           ("a", 2, "new"), ("a", 3, None)]
assert compact_all(records) == {"b": "keep"}
assert compact_all(records[:3]) == {"a": "new", "b": "keep"}
print("latest version wins; tombstone masks older data")
```

Expected output is the printed sentence; Python 3 standard library only. It proves the selected version rule for these inputs, not file durability, concurrent-reader safety, or a compaction scheduler.

A practical failure scenario is nearly full storage during compaction: input files cannot be removed before safe replacement, so the engine needs temporary free space. Another is a read-heavy workload with many overlapping runs; reducing foreground write cost increased lookup work. Do not say all B-trees are slow at writes or all LSM engines are fast at reads. Compare measured access patterns, memory, write budget, and recovery behavior.
